WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.840
Daphne Gray-Grant grew up in the newspaper business.

2
00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:11.265
Her father owned a struggling weekly paper in Vancouver, Canada.

3
00:00:11.265 --> 00:00:13.365
While going to high school and college,

4
00:00:13.365 --> 00:00:16.825
she worked there until she graduated and for a few more years.

5
00:00:16.825 --> 00:00:19.140
Then, she became a senior editor at

6
00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:23.400
a large metropolitan daily where she worked for about eight years.

7
00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:26.939
Following that, she became pregnant with triplets,

8
00:00:26.939 --> 00:00:30.200
and decided she still didn't have nearly enough work.

9
00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:32.295
She wanted to be self-employed.

10
00:00:32.295 --> 00:00:35.585
So, the Publication Coach was born.

11
00:00:35.585 --> 00:00:38.750
Interestingly, Daphne says that she struggled

12
00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:41.719
with writers block during all of her years of newspapering,

13
00:00:41.719 --> 00:00:45.260
and didn't address the problem until she started her own business.

14
00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:47.710
She's also the author of the popular book,

15
00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:51.015
Eight and a Half Steps to Writing Faster, Better.

16
00:00:51.015 --> 00:00:55.975
Through her website, www.publicationcoach.com,

17
00:00:55.975 --> 00:00:59.855
she offers the weekly newsletter power writing.

18
00:00:59.855 --> 00:01:04.010
It's weekly, brief, and best of all, it's free.

19
00:01:04.010 --> 00:01:06.440
I've been a subscriber for years myself,

20
00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:08.785
and I encourage you to sign up as well.

21
00:01:08.785 --> 00:01:13.175
Meanwhile, it's my great pleasure to welcome Daphne here today.

22
00:01:13.175 --> 00:01:16.290
Daphne, you're a professional writing coach.

23
00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:20.545
Tell me about your own past struggles with writer's block.

24
00:01:20.545 --> 00:01:26.245
Well, you know, the interesting thing for me is that I struggled enormously with writing,

25
00:01:26.245 --> 00:01:31.130
even when I was a professional reporter at a daily newspaper.

26
00:01:31.130 --> 00:01:36.485
Now, you might think how can someone who works at a daily newspaper have writer's block.

27
00:01:36.485 --> 00:01:38.325
Didn't you have to write every day?

28
00:01:38.325 --> 00:01:41.090
But the thing is, I was a born editor,

29
00:01:41.090 --> 00:01:42.980
and when I worked in newspapers,

30
00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:46.360
I did a little bit of writing and mostly editing.

31
00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:49.560
I loved editing, I've always loved editing.

32
00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:53.720
The few times when I had to write when I worked for the daily newspaper,

33
00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:58.175
I found it very stressful and extremely difficult.

34
00:01:58.175 --> 00:02:01.485
Partly, I guess because I was an editor, and so,

35
00:02:01.485 --> 00:02:06.019
I was a little bit embarrassed about how slow a writer I was,

36
00:02:06.019 --> 00:02:11.690
but also because I hadn't really learned the tricks and techniques I've since learned,

37
00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:15.385
to make writing faster, easier, and better.

38
00:02:15.385 --> 00:02:18.780
Well, I think we'll be talking about some of those tricks,

39
00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:23.525
and one of the ways I think that we might talk about that is just,

40
00:02:23.525 --> 00:02:26.320
how did you teach your triplets to write?

41
00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:29.150
Did you use some of those tricks?

42
00:02:29.230 --> 00:02:33.620
Well, I taught my triplets to do many things.

43
00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:35.110
They're twenty years old now,

44
00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:38.110
and they're all at university, which is great.

45
00:02:38.110 --> 00:02:42.765
They're so different you'd barely know they were siblings, never mind triplets.

46
00:02:42.765 --> 00:02:45.850
I don't know whether it would be fair to say I taught them to write,

47
00:02:45.850 --> 00:02:51.290
but I certainly gave them some of the tricks and techniques I give my own clients,

48
00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:54.085
and they too have found them very helpful.

49
00:02:54.085 --> 00:02:57.040
Well, let's hone in on one of those tricks.

50
00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:02.630
I mean, how would you describe the difference between diffuse mode and focused mode,

51
00:03:02.630 --> 00:03:03.840
when you're thinking about,

52
00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:05.670
and when it comes to writing?

53
00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:10.040
Yeah. That's a really really important point because,

54
00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:12.140
for me the diffuse mode,

55
00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:17.575
I call that the creative or writing mode.

56
00:03:17.575 --> 00:03:23.415
The focus mode, I call the editing brain.

57
00:03:23.415 --> 00:03:27.590
One of the big mistakes that many people make when they're writing,

58
00:03:27.590 --> 00:03:31.310
is they try to write with their editing brain.

59
00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:34.280
The metaphor I like to use is, I say,

60
00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:39.829
imagine that these two different parts of your brain are like two different people,

61
00:03:39.829 --> 00:03:41.675
and now put them in a car.

62
00:03:41.675 --> 00:03:43.940
What do you know about that?

63
00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:46.560
Only one person can drive.

64
00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:51.595
So, if you're critical or focused brain is driving,

65
00:03:51.595 --> 00:03:55.130
your diffused brain is asleep in the back seat,

66
00:03:55.130 --> 00:03:58.220
and that's not what anyone wants when they're writing.

67
00:03:58.220 --> 00:04:02.180
You want your diffused brain to be active and awake,

68
00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:05.145
to be the one that's driving when you're writing.

69
00:04:05.145 --> 00:04:08.650
So, one of the techniques I like to use to help

70
00:04:08.650 --> 00:04:12.785
people engage their diffused brain, is mind mapping.

71
00:04:12.785 --> 00:04:15.670
Now, the important thing about mind mapping is,

72
00:04:15.670 --> 00:04:18.380
its a little bit different from concept mapping.

73
00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:22.685
I don't know too much about concept mapping because I'm not an academic myself,

74
00:04:22.685 --> 00:04:26.640
but mind mapping which is also called clustering,

75
00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:29.850
some people may know it by that name instead,

76
00:04:29.850 --> 00:04:34.180
is where you take a piece of paper and you turn it sideways,

77
00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:36.200
and that's really really important.

78
00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:39.260
Having the paper sideways is essential,

79
00:04:39.260 --> 00:04:40.640
because what that does is that,

80
00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:42.655
kind of liberates your brain,

81
00:04:42.655 --> 00:04:43.880
and it says, oh,

82
00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:45.930
I can go off in any direction.

83
00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:51.370
If you have a paper in the sort of standard eight and a half by 11 way,

84
00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:54.920
that's going to make your brain think that you need to write a list,

85
00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:57.500
or worse an outline.

86
00:04:57.500 --> 00:05:01.265
I really encourage students in particular,

87
00:05:01.265 --> 00:05:03.875
not to outline before writing.

88
00:05:03.875 --> 00:05:06.070
I know this flies in the face of

89
00:05:06.070 --> 00:05:10.055
what many grade ten English teachers will counsel you to do.

90
00:05:10.055 --> 00:05:12.545
But the thing is, if you think about it,

91
00:05:12.545 --> 00:05:16.150
outlining engages your focused brain.

92
00:05:16.150 --> 00:05:20.480
That's the part of the brain that's good at spelling, and grammar,

93
00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:25.950
and alphabetical order in this sort of very specific tasks.

94
00:05:25.950 --> 00:05:28.635
When you're writing, you want to create,

95
00:05:28.635 --> 00:05:30.450
you want to think of things,

96
00:05:30.450 --> 00:05:32.600
you want to make new connections,

97
00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:35.455
and that's where you really need the diffused brain.

98
00:05:35.455 --> 00:05:37.430
So, to mind map,

99
00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:38.800
take that piece of paper,

100
00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:43.040
turn it sideways and write the topic in the center of the page,

101
00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:45.525
then draw a circle around it.

102
00:05:45.525 --> 00:05:49.070
Once you've drawn that circle around it,

103
00:05:49.070 --> 00:05:53.325
then, just write whatever springs into your brain.

104
00:05:53.325 --> 00:05:55.060
The thing I also like to say,

105
00:05:55.060 --> 00:05:57.220
and people find this a little bit gross sometimes,

106
00:05:57.220 --> 00:06:00.839
but I say it's kind of like vomiting onto the page.

107
00:06:00.839 --> 00:06:04.120
So, don't criticize yourself,

108
00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:08.470
don't second guess any of those ideas that occurs to your brain,

109
00:06:08.470 --> 00:06:09.955
then write it down.

110
00:06:09.955 --> 00:06:12.820
So, I did a mind map some years ago,

111
00:06:12.820 --> 00:06:16.630
and I use this when I'm teaching mind mapping all the time,

112
00:06:16.630 --> 00:06:19.060
and I did it on the first day of school.

113
00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:21.760
I don't mean I wrote the mind map on the first day of school.

114
00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:24.390
I wrote the mind map about the first day of school.

115
00:06:24.390 --> 00:06:26.980
So, what I wrote in the center of the page was,

116
00:06:26.980 --> 00:06:28.645
first day of school.

117
00:06:28.645 --> 00:06:31.330
Part of the reason I picked this topic,

118
00:06:31.330 --> 00:06:37.360
was because it breaks very neatly into some obvious marking points.

119
00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:39.170
So, one is grade one,

120
00:06:39.170 --> 00:06:41.950
one is high school, one is university.

121
00:06:41.950 --> 00:06:45.610
So, you can have different first days of school tied to those events.

122
00:06:45.610 --> 00:06:47.830
So, then I wrote those down,

123
00:06:47.830 --> 00:06:50.800
and you'll see I've marked them with little cross hatches.

124
00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:52.840
I did that after the fact, just so that,

125
00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:55.775
when you look at the mind map you can see this easily.

126
00:06:55.775 --> 00:06:57.820
Then, if you look,

127
00:06:57.820 --> 00:07:04.115
you'll see next to the one I've marked grade one in the top right hand corner,

128
00:07:04.115 --> 00:07:06.490
I've written the words, bee sting.

129
00:07:06.490 --> 00:07:11.430
Now, what does a bee sting have to do with the first day of grade one?

130
00:07:11.430 --> 00:07:15.240
Well, when I went to grade 1,

131
00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:19.520
first day of school I'm standing in the school yard with my mother,

132
00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:22.760
and I got stung on the lip by a bee.

133
00:07:22.760 --> 00:07:26.235
I will never ever forget that moment.

134
00:07:26.235 --> 00:07:29.480
For me, first day of school will always be

135
00:07:29.480 --> 00:07:33.270
linked with being stung by a bee right there on the lip.

136
00:07:33.270 --> 00:07:35.730
So, all I have to write is two words,

137
00:07:35.730 --> 00:07:38.380
bee sting, and everything comes back to me.

138
00:07:38.380 --> 00:07:40.290
I can picture the spot,

139
00:07:40.290 --> 00:07:44.270
the exact spot in the playground where I was standing.

140
00:07:44.270 --> 00:07:48.160
I can remember the crabby old nun who came up to me and said,

141
00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:50.555
"What are you making such a big deal about?

142
00:07:50.555 --> 00:07:52.365
Because I was crying.

143
00:07:52.365 --> 00:07:54.605
So, it's almost like a movie.

144
00:07:54.605 --> 00:07:57.005
I can play in my own brain,

145
00:07:57.005 --> 00:07:59.560
just based on those two words.

146
00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:04.610
Now, one of the questions I often get when I talk about this is people say, "Well,

147
00:08:04.610 --> 00:08:08.750
I can see how mind mapping would be very useful for fiction,

148
00:08:08.750 --> 00:08:12.440
and I could see how it might even be useful for memory,

149
00:08:12.440 --> 00:08:15.552
but, what does it have to do with non-fiction.

150
00:08:15.552 --> 00:08:19.670
I have to say that I use mind mapping everyday of

151
00:08:19.670 --> 00:08:24.385
my writing life and I have never written any fiction ever.

152
00:08:24.385 --> 00:08:27.450
So, I use it entirely for non-fiction.

153
00:08:27.450 --> 00:08:32.115
What you can do is when you interview someone and you take your notes,

154
00:08:32.115 --> 00:08:35.280
then you put the notes aside and you do

155
00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:38.575
the mind map based on your memory of that interview,

156
00:08:38.575 --> 00:08:41.450
and the things that are most interesting,

157
00:08:41.450 --> 00:08:44.825
I guarantee your brain will remember.

158
00:08:44.825 --> 00:08:46.900
Now, for university students,

159
00:08:46.900 --> 00:08:48.420
the issue is going to be a bit different

160
00:08:48.420 --> 00:08:52.880
because it's less likely you're going to be interviewing a bunch of people.

161
00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:55.345
It's more likely you're going to be reading a bunch of

162
00:08:55.345 --> 00:08:58.565
books but the same principle applies.

163
00:08:58.565 --> 00:09:02.050
If there is something that is really interesting to

164
00:09:02.050 --> 00:09:05.605
you and important and integral to your research,

165
00:09:05.605 --> 00:09:08.790
you're likely to remember that and the things that are

166
00:09:08.790 --> 00:09:12.090
most important are going to float to the top of your memory.

167
00:09:12.090 --> 00:09:16.510
So, you will remember them and then you can mind map about them.

168
00:09:16.510 --> 00:09:20.595
This is very interesting because it's related to

169
00:09:20.595 --> 00:09:25.720
the techniques that brilliant Nobel Prize winning neuroscientist,

170
00:09:25.720 --> 00:09:29.130
Santiago Ramón y Cajal, used when he was trying

171
00:09:29.130 --> 00:09:33.860
to really understand what was going on at an anatomical level.

172
00:09:33.860 --> 00:09:37.760
He would look very carefully at the cells he was watching in

173
00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:43.330
his microscope and then he would turn and he would try to draw them.

174
00:09:43.330 --> 00:09:51.870
He would draw and re-draw until he had abstracted the key chunks that stuck in

175
00:09:51.870 --> 00:09:55.245
his memory from when he was looking at them with

176
00:09:55.245 --> 00:09:59.650
the microscope versus when he was trying to draw them on the page.

177
00:09:59.650 --> 00:10:04.205
So, in some sense, it seems what you're doing is to try and abstract

178
00:10:04.205 --> 00:10:07.480
the key ideas that have somehow crystallized in

179
00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:11.435
your mind through using the mind-mapping technique.

180
00:10:11.435 --> 00:10:15.950
That's a really really good way of putting it.

181
00:10:15.950 --> 00:10:19.855
It interests me in terms of education these days.

182
00:10:19.855 --> 00:10:25.310
I think we are down playing a bit the value of memory.

183
00:10:25.310 --> 00:10:30.475
I don't know about you, but I have all my phone numbers entered into my iPhone.

184
00:10:30.475 --> 00:10:33.410
I don't remember a phone number anymore to save

185
00:10:33.410 --> 00:10:37.870
my life and people don't memorize poetry anymore.

186
00:10:37.870 --> 00:10:41.220
There's all sorts of things that used to be part of

187
00:10:41.220 --> 00:10:46.540
the regular day to day life and regular school life that aren't there so much anymore.

188
00:10:46.540 --> 00:10:52.610
So, I think we're a little bit wary of forcing ourselves to rely on our memory at all,

189
00:10:52.610 --> 00:10:56.280
but the thing that strikes me about mind mapping is that if

190
00:10:56.280 --> 00:11:00.195
something is interesting or important to you,

191
00:11:00.195 --> 00:11:01.670
you're going to remember it.

192
00:11:01.670 --> 00:11:04.925
At least you're going to remember well enough to mind map it.

193
00:11:04.925 --> 00:11:09.325
Then, of course when you get to the point where you have to do citations,

194
00:11:09.325 --> 00:11:11.220
of course you have to look it up.

195
00:11:11.220 --> 00:11:16.330
You have to get that 100 percent right or you're quoting someone else,

196
00:11:16.330 --> 00:11:18.630
you need to check it word for word.

197
00:11:18.630 --> 00:11:23.580
But for doing the mind map for inspiring yourself to write,

198
00:11:23.580 --> 00:11:27.160
you're not needing that kind of 100 percent accuracy,

199
00:11:27.160 --> 00:11:29.435
you're just needing the inspiration.

200
00:11:29.435 --> 00:11:32.230
That's what mind mapping is so valuable for.

201
00:11:32.230 --> 00:11:37.545
I couldn't agree more and I also couldn't agree more about the importance of memory.

202
00:11:37.545 --> 00:11:43.405
I think what we've done is mistakenly by thinking, well,

203
00:11:43.405 --> 00:11:47.630
memorization can be sometimes a bad thing

204
00:11:47.630 --> 00:11:51.615
if you're just sitting there memorizing things without understanding them.

205
00:11:51.615 --> 00:11:54.025
But, we've thrown the baby out with the bath water,

206
00:11:54.025 --> 00:11:59.205
we've said memorization is always bad and that's actually not true.

207
00:11:59.205 --> 00:12:01.805
I mean, great poets have said,

208
00:12:01.805 --> 00:12:05.000
"Memorize a poem because it helps you

209
00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:09.755
to understand the underlying meaning much more deeply."

210
00:12:09.755 --> 00:12:14.025
In the same way, when you memorize for example an equation,

211
00:12:14.025 --> 00:12:18.660
it can help you understand that poetic under tone

212
00:12:18.660 --> 00:12:23.340
that underlies the equation and that's often the lost.

213
00:12:23.340 --> 00:12:26.335
But I have to bring out something

214
00:12:26.335 --> 00:12:29.985
you've alluded to in the past and that's simply the idea of,

215
00:12:29.985 --> 00:12:35.785
when you're trying to get into that diffuse mode and I'm saying this

216
00:12:35.785 --> 00:12:41.370
a little bit metaphorically because we haven't truly analyzed what's going on.

217
00:12:41.370 --> 00:12:44.530
But that mode where you're stepping back a little

218
00:12:44.530 --> 00:12:48.615
bit and you're not focusing completely and intently,

219
00:12:48.615 --> 00:12:52.800
and you're trying to get something down on a page that

220
00:12:52.800 --> 00:12:56.760
sometimes in order to avoid that editorial mode,

221
00:12:56.760 --> 00:13:03.545
the best thing to do is to cover up your screen with a towel or something like that.

222
00:13:03.545 --> 00:13:06.840
That helps prevent you from going back and trying to

223
00:13:06.840 --> 00:13:10.805
edit what you just wrote and just get it out onto the page.

224
00:13:10.805 --> 00:13:17.790
Absolutely, you know that is the number one mistake that most students.

225
00:13:18.160 --> 00:13:21.225
You don't have to be a student to make this mistake.

226
00:13:21.225 --> 00:13:25.730
Most beginning writers make is they edit while they write and I like to

227
00:13:25.730 --> 00:13:30.910
say that that's kind of like trying to clear the table while you're still eating dinner.

228
00:13:30.910 --> 00:13:33.905
It just doesn't work very well.

229
00:13:33.905 --> 00:13:37.290
One of the tricks I suggest to people if they've developed

230
00:13:37.290 --> 00:13:41.755
this habit is that they turn off their monitor while they're writing.

231
00:13:41.755 --> 00:13:45.815
So, of course you need to be a touch typist to be able to do that.

232
00:13:45.815 --> 00:13:47.680
But, if you are a touch typist,

233
00:13:47.680 --> 00:13:50.720
then turn off your monitor and if that feels to drastic or

234
00:13:50.720 --> 00:13:53.910
if you have a Mac that doesn't let you turn off your monitor separately,

235
00:13:53.910 --> 00:13:59.485
then just hang a towel over it and write without looking at what you're reading.

236
00:13:59.485 --> 00:14:06.225
I wrote for probably more than 40 years while

237
00:14:06.225 --> 00:14:12.550
I edited constantly while I wrote and that was so dysfunctional and so bad for me.

238
00:14:12.550 --> 00:14:18.200
It slowed me down so much and made writing so difficult and traumatic.

239
00:14:18.200 --> 00:14:23.455
That I finally made the decision that I had to break that habit.

240
00:14:23.455 --> 00:14:26.890
So, I started turning off my monitor.

241
00:14:26.890 --> 00:14:29.985
I tried some other things as well that also really helped.

242
00:14:29.985 --> 00:14:34.115
There's a great app on the Internet called write or die.

243
00:14:34.115 --> 00:14:36.590
So, it's writeordie.com.

244
00:14:36.590 --> 00:14:39.740
If you scroll down that screen a little bit,

245
00:14:39.740 --> 00:14:44.485
then you'll get to an area where you can enter the number of words you want to write and

246
00:14:44.485 --> 00:14:49.830
enter a time limit for yourself and then you press a button that says,

247
00:14:49.830 --> 00:14:50.990
I can't remember what it says,

248
00:14:50.990 --> 00:14:56.880
but something like start and then you get a blank screen and you start to write.

249
00:14:57.880 --> 00:15:02.860
What inevitably happens is we will write a few words and then we

250
00:15:02.860 --> 00:15:08.790
stop and we look off and stare into space while we're trying to plan what to write next.

251
00:15:08.790 --> 00:15:15.940
What happens is the software notices we're not typing and after X number of seconds,

252
00:15:15.940 --> 00:15:20.280
I think it's about 10 seconds of not moving the keyboard at all,

253
00:15:20.280 --> 00:15:25.380
then the screen starts to pinken and I like to compare it to a blush.

254
00:15:25.380 --> 00:15:27.935
Because the screen's embarrassed for you.

255
00:15:27.935 --> 00:15:33.125
It gets pinker and pinker and pinker the longer you go without writing.

256
00:15:33.125 --> 00:15:36.170
I haven't timed this in a long time so I can't

257
00:15:36.170 --> 00:15:39.140
remember exactly the time limit it gives you.

258
00:15:39.140 --> 00:15:41.830
Let's say it's 20 seconds.

259
00:15:41.830 --> 00:15:45.275
If you've gone 20 seconds without writing at all,

260
00:15:45.275 --> 00:15:48.140
then you get a loud punishing noise.

261
00:15:48.140 --> 00:15:50.150
So, its either a crying baby,

262
00:15:50.150 --> 00:15:53.640
a car alarm, or '70s disco music.

263
00:15:53.640 --> 00:15:56.425
I'll leave it to you to decide which is worse,

264
00:15:56.425 --> 00:16:00.029
but it's a very clever technique

265
00:16:00.029 --> 00:16:04.660
for teaching you to break the habit of editing while you write.

266
00:16:04.660 --> 00:16:10.695
I've used Write or Die myself and I think it does work very, very well.

267
00:16:10.695 --> 00:16:16.980
I love this approach of separating your editing from your actual writing.

268
00:16:16.980 --> 00:16:21.005
For me, what I sometimes find is when I think of

269
00:16:21.005 --> 00:16:25.585
something and I'm about to put it down and then I think, that's terrible.

270
00:16:25.585 --> 00:16:27.350
I can't write that.

271
00:16:27.350 --> 00:16:32.380
Then I'm editing in my head before I'm writing and when I stop that,

272
00:16:32.380 --> 00:16:33.660
when I just say, "Okay,

273
00:16:33.660 --> 00:16:34.965
I have got to get that out.

274
00:16:34.965 --> 00:16:36.040
Hey, that's terrible."

275
00:16:36.040 --> 00:16:37.270
But that's the way it is,

276
00:16:37.270 --> 00:16:39.530
it's going to come out on the page terrible.

277
00:16:39.530 --> 00:16:42.710
I'm so surprised later when I looked back and I find,

278
00:16:42.710 --> 00:16:43.865
hey, you know what?

279
00:16:43.865 --> 00:16:46.120
That actually wasn't as terrible as I thought.

280
00:16:46.120 --> 00:16:50.095
In fact, it got me started and it's pretty good.

281
00:16:50.095 --> 00:16:55.170
Yes. That happens to so many people.

282
00:16:55.170 --> 00:16:59.720
I have to tell you, I have taught writing for so many years now.

283
00:16:59.720 --> 00:17:02.600
I can tell you that when I talk to people about

284
00:17:02.600 --> 00:17:07.095
that nasty little editing voice that's in the back of all our brains,

285
00:17:07.095 --> 00:17:11.060
we all have that voice and it says the same thing to all of us.

286
00:17:11.060 --> 00:17:12.500
It says things like;

287
00:17:12.500 --> 00:17:14.165
this is no good,

288
00:17:14.165 --> 00:17:17.910
my boss or teacher is going to hate this,

289
00:17:17.910 --> 00:17:22.080
this is boring, this is really really bad.

290
00:17:22.080 --> 00:17:28.545
That voice says really negative essentially unhelpful things to all of us.

291
00:17:28.545 --> 00:17:30.485
We're all in the same boat.

292
00:17:30.485 --> 00:17:36.015
So, what I like to do is I like to talk back to that voice and I say things like,

293
00:17:36.015 --> 00:17:37.840
could you shut up for a minute?

294
00:17:37.840 --> 00:17:40.195
I don't have time to talk right now.

295
00:17:40.195 --> 00:17:44.780
I will talk to you when I'm editing but I'm writing right now.

296
00:17:44.780 --> 00:17:46.160
I'm too busy.

297
00:17:46.160 --> 00:17:50.070
If you can be conscious of that voice and talk back

298
00:17:50.070 --> 00:17:53.835
to it rather than just let it yammer in your ear,

299
00:17:53.835 --> 00:17:55.055
then you're better off.

300
00:17:55.055 --> 00:17:57.830
So, I think the number one technique for dealing

301
00:17:57.830 --> 00:18:01.760
with that is just recognizing that it's happening.

302
00:18:01.760 --> 00:18:07.600
Recognizing that it happens to everyone and taking some steps to deal with it.

303
00:18:07.600 --> 00:18:10.340
Well, I thank you so much Daphne.

304
00:18:10.340 --> 00:18:15.600
This was very useful and a fantastic reminder even for me.

305
00:18:15.600 --> 00:18:21.820
I want to encourage everyone out there to please go ahead and explore signing up

306
00:18:21.820 --> 00:18:28.805
for Daphne's free newsletter which is at www.publicationcoach.com.

307
00:18:28.805 --> 00:18:33.590
I'm a subscriber as well and maybe even take a look at her great book,

308
00:18:33.590 --> 00:18:36.110
Eight and a Half Steps to Writing Faster,

309
00:18:36.110 --> 00:18:42.055
Better or Daphne's email course which is called Extreme Writing Makeover.

310
00:18:42.055 --> 00:18:46.890
So, thank you so much again Daphne and we'll see you on the flip-side.

311
00:18:46.890 --> 00:18:49.680
Thanks Barb, nice talking to you.